To create a Chrome extension that saves the contents of the current webpage, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. **Set up your project structure**: Create a folder for your extension and inside it, create two files: `manifest.json` and `content.js`.

2. **Create the manifest file** (`manifest.json`): This file contains metadata about your extension.

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Save Page Content",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Saves the content of the current webpage.",
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "storage"],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": {
      "16": "icons/icon16.png",
      "48": "icons/icon48.png",
      "128": "icons/icon128.png"
    }
  },
  "icons": {
    "16": "icons/icon16.png",
    "48": "icons/icon48.png",
    "128": "icons/icon128.png"
  }
}
```

3. **Create the background script** (`background.js`): This script will handle saving the page content when triggered by the popup.

```javascript
chrome.action.onClicked.addListener((tab) => {
  chrome.scripting.executeScript({
    target: { tabId: tab.id },
    files: ['content.js']
  });
});
```

4. **Create the content script** (`content.js`): This script will extract the HTML content from the current page.

```javascript
// Extract the entire body content
const bodyContent = document.body.innerHTML;

// Save the content to local storage or send it back to the background script
chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ action: 'saveContent', data: bodyContent }, (response) => {
  console.log('Content saved:', response);
});
```

5. **Create the popup UI** (`popup.html`): A simple HTML file with a button to trigger the save action.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Save Page Content</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; padding: 10px; }
    button { padding: 10px; width: 100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <button id="saveButton">Save Current Page Content</button>

  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

6. **Add JavaScript logic to the popup** (`popup.js`): Handle the click event on the button to trigger the save action.

```javascript
document.getElementById('saveButton').addEventListener('click', () => {
  chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, (tabs) => {
    chrome.scripting.executeScript({
      target: { tabId: tabs[0].id },
      files: ['content.js']
    });
  });
});
```

7. **Load your extension into Chrome**: Go to `chrome://extensions/`, enable Developer Mode, and click "Load unpacked". Select the directory where your extension is located.

This setup provides a basic framework for an extension that can save the content of the current webpage. You may want to enhance this further by adding features like saving to cloud storage, providing options for different formats (e.g., plain text, PDF), etc.

