To extract hashtags from a given text in C#, you can use regular expressions (regex). Here's an example of how to do it:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string inputText = "This is a sample text with #hashtags and @mentions like #CSharp and #Regex.";

        // Define the regex pattern for hashtags
        string hashtagPattern = @"#\w+";

        // Use Regex.Matches to find all matches in the input text
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(inputText, hashtagPattern);

        // Iterate through the matches and print them
        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
        }
    }
}
```

In this code:
- The `inputText` variable contains the text from which you want to extract hashtags.
- The `hashtagPattern` variable defines the regex pattern that matches any word starting with a hash symbol (`#`) followed by one or more word characters (`\w+`).
- The `Regex.Matches` method finds all occurrences of the pattern in the input text.
- Finally, we iterate over the `MatchCollection` and print each matched hashtag.

When you run this program, it will output:
```
#hashtags
#CSharp
#Regex
```

This approach ensures that only valid hashtags are extracted from the text.

To add functionality to the button so it sends an HTTP POST request with the user's name and a fixed user key, you can use JavaScript (or jQuery if you prefer). Here’s how you can do it:

### Using Vanilla JavaScript

First, ensure your HTML includes a script tag at the end of the body or within the head section for better performance.

```html
<script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
    document.querySelector('.btn-primary').addEventListener('click', function(event) {
        event.preventDefault(); // Prevent form submission if inside a form

        const userName = document.getElementById('user_name').value;
        const userKey = 'd2db5ec4-6e7a-11ee-b962-0242ac120002';

        fetch(`http://localhost:5000/api/users/${userName}/devices/${userKey}`, {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: {
                'Content-Type': 'application/json'
            }
        })
        .then(response => response.json())
        .then(data => console.log(data))
        .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));
    });
});
</script>
```

### Using jQuery

If you prefer using jQuery, include the library in your project first by adding `<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>` before your custom script.

Then, modify your script like this:

```html
<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
    $('.btn-primary').on('click', function(event) {
        event.preventDefault(); // Prevent form submission if inside a form

        var userName = $('#user_name').val();
        var userKey = 'd2db5ec4-6e7a-11ee-b962-0242ac120002';

        $.ajax({
            url: `http://localhost:5000/api/users/${userName}/devices/${userKey}`,
            type: 'POST',
            success: function(data) {
                console.log(data);
            },
            error: function(error) {
                console.error('Error:', error);
            }
        });
    });
});
</script>
```

