To achieve similar functionality to CSS Container Queries using Jetpack Compose, you can leverage `Modifier` properties such as `fillMaxWidth`, `wrapContentHeight`, or other layout modifiers along with conditional logic based on the size of the parent container.

Here’s an example of how you might implement this:

```kotlin
import androidx.compose.foundation.layout.*
import androidx.compose.material3.Text
import androidx.compose.runtime.Composable
import androidx.compose.ui.Modifier
import androidx.compose.ui.unit.dp

@Composable
fun ResponsiveChild(modifier: Modifier = Modifier) {
    // Example condition for changing behavior based on width
    val isNarrowScreen = modifier.width() < 400.dp

    Box(
        modifier = modifier
            .padding(16.dp)
            .then(if (isNarrowScreen) Modifier.fillMaxWidth() else Modifier.wrapContentWidth())
    ) {
        Text(text = "Hello, World!")
    }
}

@Composable
fun ParentContainer() {
    Column(
        modifier = Modifier
            .fillMaxSize()
            .padding(16.dp),
        horizontalAlignment = Alignment.CenterHorizontally,
        verticalArrangement = Arrangement.Center
    ) {
        ResponsiveChild(Modifier.weight(1f))
    }
}
```

In this example:
- The `ResponsiveChild` composable checks if the current width is less than 400 dp.
- Depending on whether the screen is narrow or not, it adjusts its own width using either `fillMaxWidth` or `wrapContentWidth`.
- This approach allows you to dynamically adjust the layout based on the available space within the parent container.

This method provides a way to mimic some aspects of container queries by adjusting the layout behavior based on the dimensions of the parent container.
